WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.060
Film Courage: Okay, so the Unwritten Rules
of Hollywood, your book [HOLLYWOOD GAME PLAN:

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.060
How To Land A Job In Film, TV, and Digital
Entertainment].

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:07.569
Without giving away too much, from those who
have read the book, what are some takeaways

00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:10.280
where they were completely surprised?

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:15.240
Whether it was a chapter, whether it was a
tip or two that you gave?

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:21.830
Carole Kirschner: I think that some of the
things that people came away with that they

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:27.449
were not expecting was that there really are
people that want to help you.

00:00:27.449 --> 00:00:33.720
Even though there’s some…there’s some
people that would rather eat dirt than help

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:34.720
you.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.850
Most people, somebody helped them, so they’re
willing to help.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:42.810
I have a chapter on mentoring and how to treat
your mentor right.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:47.200
And I think a lot of people didn’t understand
that you don’t go up to somebody and say

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.340
“Will you be my mentor?”

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:53.080
A relationship has to develop and then how
to make the ask.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:58.960
I talk about the Hollywood Ask, which is a
particular kind of an ask so that you don’t

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.610
back somebody into the corner.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:08.770
What people say is and these are people who
have also gone to film school is “I didn’t

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.600
understand how the business worked.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.860
And now I understand what I should be doing,
what I shouldn’t be doing, how I should

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:17.990
be talking about myself.”

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.910
The whole thing about networking (and I hate
the word networking) but how you connect with

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:29.410
people in an authentic way so that a real
relationship forms and how that’s important

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.970
(you don’t have to be that smarmy networker).

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:37.909
Film Courage: Which chapter do you wish you’d
read when you first decided (I know you grew

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:39.940
up here in Los Angeles) but that you wanted
to be in the business?

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.700
Which chapter [in Carole’s own book] did
you say “You know what, I could have used

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.160
that 10 years ago.”

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.479
Carole Kirschner: That’s a really interesting
question.

00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:49.479
Which of the chapters?

00:01:49.479 --> 00:01:53.180
Now I have to think back to the chapters because
it was awhile back.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:59.550
I think it was about how to prepare for meeting
and how to follow up because I didn’t know,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:05.000
I didn’t know what you say to people, I
didn’t know how informal it was or how formal

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.000
it was.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.750
I have a chapter about what to wear.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:13.780
I probably would have appreciated knowing
that because you don’t know how formal or

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:18.620
informal you should be in what circumstance.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:22.920
And also, how to connect with people.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:29.540
How to meet people, what to do when you meet
them, I wish I had known that stuff.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:36.030
But mostly how to behave so I didn’t put
my foot in my mouth, so I didn’t make big

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:36.530
mistakes.

